    "product_id": "8e7e66ea-d972-4a59-af0b-9e26de5bc1ef",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 170536\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1036 PM MST Wed Sep 16 2026\n\n.UPDATE...06Z Aviation Discussion\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Scattered to widespread showers and storms are forecast to\n  continue today across Arizona, with areas of high rainfall \n  amounts leading to flash flooding.\n\n- A Flood Watch remains in effect through early tonight across \n  much of southern Arizona. \n\n- Shower and thunderstorm chances will greatly diminish by\n  Thursday and come to an end starting Friday with much drier \n  air moving in and lasting through at least this weekend. \n\n- Below normal temperatures are expected today across south-\n  central Arizona, before warming back to to slightly above \n  normal levels by the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\n\nRAP40 500mb height analysis shows broad troughing over Western \nCONUS, with the low centered over Northern California, and ridging \nover the southern Gulf States. This places the Desert SW sandwiched \nbetween these two larger features, keeping flow aloft mostly out of \nthe south-southwest, advecting moisture into the region from Northern \nMexico. More impactful, a well defined low pressure circulation is \nseen on satellite along the west coast of Sonora, MX. This feature \nis forecasted to continue moving straight north up through AZ likely \nthis afternoon through tonight and help provide the lift that has \nbeen somewhat lacking. The atmosphere is primed with ample moisture, \nwith 1000-700mb mixing ratios from this morning's 12Z sounding at 14 \ng/kg and PWATs at 1.90\", upwards of 200% of normal. With this \nmoisture in place the lift from the previously mentioned low will be \nbe strengthened by the upper level jet to the region's north \nproviding extra support. Models are forecasting MUCAPE to be \ngenerally between 1500-2000 J/kg this afternoon, however, some \nmodels are showing support for upwards of 2500 J/kg, with CIN below \n-100 J/kg. With any decent lift, this would make for a favorable \nenvironment for storms to develop and continue, possible even \ntraining leading to heavy rainfall, especially this afternoon and \ninto the later evening hours. \n\nA Flood Watch is currently in effect for south-central AZ and will \ncontinue through 11 PM MST tonight. The watch was issued largely due \nto the synoptic and mesoscale setup being supportive of scattered to \nwidespread showers and storms capable of producing 1-hr rain rates \nup around 1-2\" and higher over shorter time frames, especially in \nthe eastern high terrain. So far this morning, localized areas in \nthe eastern Phoenix, the northern Foothills and eastern high terrain \nhave received between 1.5\"-2.75\". Hi-res modeling continues to show \nadditional QPF upwards of 1-2.5\" over a large portion of south-\ncentral AZ, and there are even high end amounts up to around 3\"-4\", \nalthough chances are limited, and isolated in nature. With \neverything coming together and starting to develop as we head into \nthe afternoon hours WPC Day 1 ERO guidance remains as a Moderate \nrisk for all of Gila County and most of Maricopa County. A Slight \nERO risk remains from far western Maricopa and westwards near to \nAZ/CA state border. The last time a Moderate Risk covered this area \nwas back in 2022.\n\nToday will be the peak of the activity as the low circulation will \nmove north of the area by late tonight, but a narrow axis of showers \nand storms may linger into the overnight hours, most likely toward \neastern AZ. Once the system fully exits the region the environment \nwill be too overworked, and subsidence increases on the back side of \nthe low. This will result in drier air beginning to advent into the \nreign with west to southwesterly flow aloft. These conditions will \nlimit showers and storms mostly to northern AZ high terrain on \nThursday, fallowed by further drying by Friday, limiting showers \nacross even the higher terrain, and 1000-700 mb mixing ratios drop \nto 8 g/kg or below, and PWATs will also decrease to sub 1.00\".  \n\nTemperatures this afternoon may struggle to reach 90 degrees across \nsouth-central AZ, with ample cloud cover and rain around, while \nareas further west, with more sun, still warm into the middle 90s to \naround 100 degrees. Temperatures will warm back up by the end of the \nweek, but only back to around seasonal levels, with highs around 100 \ndegrees across the lower deserts, as broad troughing remain in place \nacross much of the west.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\n\nThe monsoon pattern may be done after today, shifting into the\ntransitional season with more frequent troughs. Dry air will \ncontinue to spread across the region through this weekend with \nPWATs forecast to drop below normal, as low as 0.4-0.7\" across the\nlower deserts. The will inhibit any chance at rain. The broad\ntrough moving through the West, helping drive the westerly flow\nand dry air, is expected to shift east into the Plains by the\nstart of next week. Global ensemble guidance then shows an upper\nlow moving out of the Pacific toward the SoCal coast by the end of\nthe day Monday. There is still plenty of uncertainty regarding\nthis low this far out, but it may be what helps eventually drive\nmoisture back up in the deserts and drive the next chance at rain.\nAverage to slightly above average temperatures are forecast this\nweekend through the first half of next week with this forecast,\nwith afternoon highs around to slightly above 100 degrees. With\nthe drier air coming in, the morning lows will finally be more\npleasant, consistently dropping into the 70s for most lower \ndesert locations.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0535Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nLingering SHRA activity and associated lower CIGs and reduced VIS\nwill be the main aviation concerns during in the near term before\nthe region dries out overnight. It appears that most, if not all,\nrainfall activity should exit the area by 09Z, but forecast \nguidance has been quite poor over the past 12 hours or so, \ntherefore, SHRA beyond this timeframe cannot be completely ruled \nout. Outside of rainfall influence winds, directions should favor \nan easterly component through Thursday morning. By the afternoon, \nconfidence in prevailing flow decreases, but it seems likely that \na full west switch will not take place. Rather, VRB conditions or \neven a continuation of easterly flow will occupy the afternoon to \nearly evening timeframe. SCT-BKN low CIGs near 4-6 kft will be \npresent through the next several hours before lifting and \neventually clearing after starting after sunrise \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the next 24 hours.\nWinds at each terminal will favor an southeast/south-southeast\ncomponent tonight ahead of an extended window of VRB conditions.\nSSE flow will become established at KBLH by late morning, while\nwesterly flow will eventually take over at KIPL later in the\nafternoon. FEW-SCT Low cloud bases around 4-6 kft will develop \nacross the region tonight, but should dissipate prior to the \nafternoon.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nPeriods of scattered to widespread showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected to continue today, focusing mostly across the eastern \ndistricts this afternoon through this evening, however widespread\nactivity over much of the lower deserts can be expected. There \nwill be a heavy rainfall and flood threat with the activity. \nMoisture levels will be elevated most areas, highest across \neastern districts, where minimum RH this afternoon will be around \n50-60%. Minimum RHs will be lowest across southeast CA, with \nvalues around 20-30%. A drying trend will begin Thursday with \nconditions becoming very dry by this weekend. Minimum RHs by \nSaturday dip to 10-25% across the lower deserts (lowest across \nwestern districts). Overnight recoveries will still be good across\neastern districts this weekend at 40-60%, while western districts\nhover around 25-45%. The drier air moving in will eliminate rain \nchances by Friday, with low chances in Gila County Thursday \nafternoon. Winds will favor the south to southeast over much of \nthe area through Wednesday with gusts of 20-30 mph at times across\nsouthwest AZ and southeast CA. In the following days winds will \nreturn to a more typical diurnal with modest afternoon upslope \ngustiness. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Flood Watch until 11 PM MST this evening for AZZ531-533-534-\n     536>563.\n\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan/Benedict\nLONG TERM...Benedict\nAVIATION...RW\nFIRE WEATHER...Ryan/Benedict\n",
